why: operator instruction 2026-09-08 — make every benchmark ask US and
  the COMPETITOR the same question, then re-run and rewrite the
  documentation. This session found SEVEN lanes that did not, and each
  read as "we are slow": §4b's fork/join row (our outside-the-runtime
  shape against kyo's inside), §16's strong-chunked pair (a number
  that never reproduced), growing-vs-adaptive (1 984 slots against
  16 384), and four the page had already recorded before I arrived.

  docs/benchmarks.md has "Lane rules" — shape, pairing, source — and
  they are DECLARED, not enforced: the §4b row broke rule 2 for weeks
  while the rule sat two screens below it. This lane audits every
  cross-library class against them mechanically rather than on
  suspicion.

what, in this order and the order matters:
  1. AUDIT every benchmark class that names a competitor, lane by
     lane, against the three rules plus a FOURTH this session
     discovered — RESOURCES: same buffer capacity, same worker count,
     same anything the runtime is given. Comparing 1 984 slots with
     16 384 is the same class of error as comparing chunked with
     elementwise.
  2. FIX what is mismatched: rename where the lane is a diagnostic,
     add the missing twin where one side has no fair spelling, and
     correct the lane where it is simply wrong.
  3. Fold in `growing-capacity-semantics`, which is a lane-fairness
     bug wearing an API hat.
  4. Profile `bench-sendbulk-inverted` — its inversion is real and its
     cause unknown, and it may yet be a pairing problem too.
  5. THEN re-run everything on a quiet box and rewrite the tables.

predict: more mismatches will turn up. Seven found in one session, in
  a file whose own rules forbid them, is not a run of bad luck — it is
  the base rate of writing comparison benchmarks. I expect the audit
  to find between two and six more, most of them on OUR side, because
  we write our lane first and the competitor's second.
  DISQUALIFYING for any fix: if a "mismatch" turns out to be a
  deliberate diagnostic with a comment saying so, it is NOT a defect
  and gets left alone — that mistake has already been made twice this
  session (bench-chunk-fold-lane, bench-producer-inverted).
